>> A2: Briefly describe how you implemented argument parsing.  How do
>> you arrange for the elements of argv[] to be in the right order?
>> How do you avoid overflowing the stack page?

process_execute() copies the command line into a fresh page (so the
caller's buffer can change or go away) and names the new thread after
the first word only, so exit messages print just the program name.

In the child, start_process() splits that copy in place with
strtok_r() using " " as the delimiter, storing a pointer to each word
in a local argv[] array.  strtok_r() skips empty tokens, so repeated
spaces count as a single separator.  argv[0] is passed to load(), and
on success push_arguments() builds the stack below PHYS_BASE:

  1. Copy the strings onto the stack, last argument first.  As each
     one is copied, argv[i] is overwritten with the string's new user
     address.
  2. Round the stack pointer down to a multiple of 4.
  3. Push a NULL pointer (argv[argc]).
  4. Push argv[argc-1] down to argv[0].  Pushing in reverse puts
     argv[0] at the lowest address, so the array reads forwards in
     memory, in the right order.
  5. Push argv (the address of argv[0]), then argc, then a fake
     return address (0).

Overflow: before writing anything, push_arguments() adds up the exact
space needed (all strings with their null bytes, at most 3 bytes of
padding, (argc + 1) argv pointers, argv, argc and the return address).
If the total is more than PGSIZE, the single stack page, it returns
false and the load fails cleanly instead of writing below the page.
The number of words is also capped at MAX_ARGS, and the command line
itself is at most one page because it is copied into one page.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A3: Why does Pintos implement strtok_r() but not strtok()?

strtok() keeps its position between calls in a hidden static
variable, so it is not reentrant.  In the kernel, several threads can
be parsing at once (e.g. two processes calling exec), and a thread can
be preempted in the middle of parsing; with strtok() they would
overwrite each other's saved position.  strtok_r() keeps the position
in a caller-supplied save_ptr, so each caller has its own state and it
is thread-safe.

>> A4: In Pintos, the kernel separates commands into a executable name
>> and arguments.  In Unix-like systems, the shell does this
>> separation.  Identify at least two advantages of the Unix approach.

1. Less code and less risk in the kernel.  Parsing untrusted strings is
   error-prone; a bug in a user-level shell only crashes the shell,
   while a bug in kernel parsing can crash or compromise the whole
   system.  It also uses less kernel time and memory.

2. Flexibility.  Each shell can offer its own syntax (quoting,
   wildcards/globbing, variables, redirection, pipes, aliases) and it
   can be changed without modifying or rebooting the kernel.  The
   kernel just receives a ready-made argv[].

3. The shell can check the command before asking the kernel to run it,
   e.g. find the executable along PATH or report a missing program,
   without creating a new process first.

