>> B2: Describe how file descriptors are associated with open files.
>> Are file descriptors unique within the entire OS or just within a
>> single process?

Each process has its own list of struct file_desc (fd_list in struct
thread).  open() calls filesys_open(), allocates a file_desc holding
the returned struct file *, gives it the number next_fd (then
increments next_fd), and appends it to the list.  Every other file
call looks up the number in the current process's list; an unknown
number fails (returns -1, or does nothing for seek/close).

File descriptors are unique only within a single process.  Two
processes can both have an fd 2 referring to different files.  Opening
the same file twice gives two different descriptors with separate
struct file objects, so they have independent positions and are
closed independently.  0 and 1 are never handed out; they are handled
specially as the keyboard and the console.  Descriptors are not
inherited by children; a child starts with an empty list.

