>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

I used the first method from the documentation: check every user
pointer before dereferencing it.

  - check_user_access(uaddr, writable) kills the process with exit
    status -1 unless the address is non-NULL, below PHYS_BASE
    (is_user_vaddr), mapped in the process's page directory
    (pagedir_get_page), and, if WRITABLE, mapped writable
    (pagedir_is_writable).

  - check_user_buffer(buf, size) checks a whole range: the first byte,
    one address in every following page, and the last byte.  It also
    rejects ranges whose end wraps around the address space.
    check_user_writable() is the same but requires writable pages; it
    is used for read(), because the kernel writes into that buffer.

  - check_user_string(str) walks the string to its null terminator,
    re-checking whenever it crosses into a new page.

  - get_arg(f, n) fetches the system call number (n = 0) and the
    arguments (n = 1..3) from the user stack at f->esp, checking all
    4 bytes of each word first, because a word can straddle a page
    boundary (the sc-boundary tests).

Once checked, the kernel reads and writes the user memory directly:
the process's page directory is active during its own system call,
so user addresses are valid kernel pointers.  Strings and buffers are
then passed straight to filesys_open(), file_read(), file_write(),
putbuf() and so on.

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data
>> to be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?

check_user_buffer() always checks the first byte and the last byte,
plus the start of every page in between.

Full page (4,096 bytes):
  - Least: 2.  If the buffer is page-aligned it lies in one page; the
    first and last byte are both checked (in the same page).
  - Greatest: 3.  If it is not aligned it spans two pages: the first
    byte, the start of the second page, and the last byte.

2 bytes:
  - Always 2 (first and last byte), whether they are in the same page
    or in two neighbouring pages.

(For read(), each check also calls pagedir_is_writable(), so double
these numbers.  Each system call argument costs another 2 checks.)

Room for improvement: the true minimum is one inspection per page
touched, i.e. 1 for a page-aligned 4,096-byte buffer or a 2-byte buffer
within one page, and 2 when either spans two pages.  Skipping the
last-byte check when it falls in a page already checked would reach
this.  The documentation's second method (only check below PHYS_BASE,
then let the MMU fault and handle it in page_fault()) needs no page
table inspections at all for valid pointers, which is why real
kernels such as Linux use it.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.

The wait system call calls process_wait(tid).  process_wait() searches
the caller's children list for a child_status with that tid.  If there
is none (not a child, or already waited for), it returns -1 at once.
Otherwise it removes the record from the list, so a second wait on the
same child returns -1, then calls sema_down(&status->exited), reads
status->exit_status, drops its reference, and returns the status.

On termination, process_exit() in the child prints
"name: exit(status)", closes its files, copies its exit_status into
its child_status, calls sema_up(&status->exited) to wake the parent,
and drops its reference.  exit_status starts at -1 and is only changed
by the exit system call, so a process killed by the kernel (bad
pointer, page fault) reports -1, as required.

Because the semaphore counts, it does not matter whether the parent
waits before or after the child exits: if the child exited first, the
semaphore is already 1 and sema_down() returns immediately.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.

Two strategies keep the code clean and leak-free.

1. Validation is factored into a few helpers (get_arg,
   check_user_buffer, check_user_writable, check_user_string) that
   never return on failure: they call sys_exit(-1), which terminates
   the process.  So each system call is written as straight-line
   code, e.g. "check the buffer; look up the fd; read", with no error
   checks scattered through it.

2. All validation happens first, before the system call takes a lock
   or allocates anything.  So at the moment a bad pointer is found,
   the process holds nothing temporary and there is nothing to undo.
   Everything a process owns long-term (open files, the executable,
   child records, its page directory) is released in one place,
   process_exit(), which runs for every exit path: a normal exit(), a
   bad pointer in a system call, or a page fault in user code (kill()
   calls thread_exit(), which calls process_exit()).

Example: write(fd, buffer, size).  syscall_handler() calls get_arg()
for the call number and the three arguments, each checked (all four
bytes) before use.  sys_write() then calls
check_user_buffer(buffer, size).  If buffer points into kernel memory
or an unmapped page, the process exits with -1 right there, before
filesys_lock is acquired.  Only after all checks pass does it look up
the fd and call file_write() while holding filesys_lock, and that
section contains no user-pointer checks, so the lock is always
released.  The fds the process had open are closed by process_exit().

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

process_execute() puts a struct load_info on its own stack, holding
the command line, a semaphore "loaded" (initially 0) and a bool
"success", and passes its address to the new thread.  After
thread_create() it calls sema_down(&info.loaded), so it blocks until
the child has finished loading.

The child, in start_process(), calls load() (holding filesys_lock),
pushes the arguments, stores the result in info->success and then
calls sema_up(&info->loaded).  After that it never touches info,
because it lives on the parent's stack.  The parent wakes up, reads
info.success, and returns -1 if loading failed (the failed child then
exits by itself) or the new tid if it succeeded.  Only a successfully
loaded child is added to the parent's children list.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

The shared child_status is reference counted: ref_cnt starts at 2 (P
and C).  Each side drops its reference exactly once, under
status_lock, and whoever brings it to 0 frees it.  So the record lives
exactly as long as at least one of them needs it.

  - P waits before C exits: P blocks in sema_down(&exited).  When C
    exits it stores its status, calls sema_up(), and drops its
    reference.  P wakes, reads the status and drops the last reference,
    freeing the record.

  - P waits after C exits: C has already stored the status, upped the
    semaphore and dropped its reference.  P's sema_down() returns
    immediately, P reads the status and frees the record.

  - P exits without waiting, before C exits: process_exit() in P drops
    P's reference to every record still in its children list.  When C
    later exits it sets the status and ups the semaphore (nobody is
    waiting, which is harmless) and drops the last reference, freeing
    the record.

  - P exits without waiting, after C exits: C already dropped its
    reference, so P's drop frees the record.

C's struct thread and page are freed by the normal thread exit path
in every case; they do not depend on the parent.  Its open files and
executable are closed in its own process_exit().

Special cases:
  - The exit status is written before sema_up(), so a woken parent
    always sees the final value.
  - A record is removed from P's list before P waits on it, so a
    second wait returns -1 immediately.
  - If the load fails, P drops its reference right away in
    process_execute() and never adds the record to its list; C drops
    the other reference when it exits.
  - Orphans are not re-parented: a grandparent never has the
    grandchild's record, so wait() on it returns -1.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

Checking pointers before using them is simple, easy to reason about,
and makes cleanup trivial: a bad pointer is always detected before a
lock is taken or memory allocated (see B6), so no resources can leak.
The page-fault method is faster for valid pointers but needs assembly
helpers (get_user/put_user), changes to page_fault(), and careful
handling of locks held at the moment of the fault.  For this project,
correctness and robustness mattered more than the small cost of a few
page-table lookups per call.

The extra writability check for read() exists because a user program
could otherwise pass a read-only page (such as its own code) as the
buffer; the kernel's write would then fault in kernel mode and panic
the whole OS.  With the check, the process is simply killed.

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

Advantages:
  - No fixed limit on open files per process; the list grows as long
    as malloc() succeeds, and open() returns -1 when it fails.
  - Small numbers instead of kernel pointers, so a user cannot forge a
    kernel address, and descriptors are naturally per process.
  - Cleanup on exit is a simple walk of the list.
  - Descriptor numbers are never reused within a process, so a stale
    fd cannot accidentally refer to a newer file.

Disadvantages:
  - Looking up an fd is O(n) in the number of open files; an array or
    hash table would be O(1).  In practice processes open few files.
  - Numbers are never reused, so a process that opens and closes files
    billions of times could overflow next_fd.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?

I kept the identity mapping: a process's pid is the tid of its single
thread.  Pintos processes have exactly one thread, so this is the
simplest correct choice and avoids an extra lookup table.  A separate
mapping would only matter for multithreaded processes, or to hide
kernel thread ids from user programs.

